<div class="ap-wrap">
  <h3>Design the end state, then subtract</h3>
  <p class="lead">The phases aren't how I designed the product, just how I shipped it. I designed Run first, the whole thing, then pulled features back out to shape Walk and Crawl. That way each smaller phase is a real slice of the same system, not something I'd have to rebuild at the next stage.</p>
  <div class="ap-tier ap-run">
    <div class="ap-tlabel"><span class="ap-tname">RUN — full end state, designed first</span><span class="ap-tside">built for scale</span></div>
    <p class="ap-tdesc">Granular QA permissions + audit, full metadata, ATC, calibration &amp; analytics.</p>
    <div class="ap-tier ap-walk">
      <div class="ap-tlabel"><span class="ap-tname">WALK — subset</span><span class="ap-tside">− ATC, − calibration, − audit workflow</span></div>
      <p class="ap-tdesc">Access + permissions with explicit roles, enhanced metadata, replicable permission sets.</p>
      <div class="ap-tier ap-crawl">
        <div class="ap-tlabel"><span class="ap-tname">CRAWL — core</span><span class="ap-tside">− granular permissions, − custom metadata</span></div>
        <p class="ap-tdesc">Basic banks, org-unit access, default metadata, enhanced filter/search.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="ap-foot">// Nothing gets rebuilt between phases. Crawl is just Run with most of it switched off.</p>
